"""Island guides: homepage picker data and the pages at /islands/<slug>.

photo is a Pexels photo ID (or a file name in photos/). make_islands.py builds the pages."""

ISLANDS = [
    {'slug': 'koh-phi-phi', 'name': 'Koh Phi Phi', 'coast': 'andaman', 'region': 'Krabi province', 'tagline': 'Cliffs, lagoons and a car-free village', 'summary': 'A small, car-free island with a busy village, lively nights and boat trips to Maya Bay. Easy to reach by ferry from Phuket or Krabi.', 'best_months': 'Nov–Apr', 'getting_there': 'Ferry from Phuket or Krabi, about 1–2 h', 'best_for': ['Snorkelling', 'Viewpoints', 'Nightlife'], 'short': 'Koh Phi Phi Travel Guide', 'title': 'Koh Phi Phi Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Koh Phi Phi travel guide: Tonsai, Loh Dalum, Long Beach and Laem Tong compared, ferries from Phuket and Krabi, Maya Bay rules, park fees and when to go.', 'category': 'Islands · Andaman coast', 'teaser': 'Where to stay on Phi Phi Don, ferries, Maya Bay rules and fees.', 'image': 'isl-koh-phi-phi', 'image_alt': 'Aerial view of the Phi Phi coast with turquoise shallows and green hills', 'stats': [('~1–2 h', 'Ferry or speedboat from Phuket or Krabi'), ('20 baht', 'Cleaning fee paid in cash at Tonsai Pier'), ('400 baht', 'National park fee for foreign adults visiting Phi Phi Leh'), ('1 Aug–30 Sep', 'Annual closure of Maya Bay')], 'body': '\n<p>Koh Phi Phi is two islands. Phi Phi Don is the larger one, where everyone stays: a narrow strip of sand between two bays, with a busy village, beach bars and limestone cliffs on either side. Phi Phi Leh, to the south, has no rooms and is visited by boat for Maya Bay and Pileh Lagoon. There are no cars, so you walk or take a longtail boat. The island suits travellers who want snorkelling, a viewpoint hike and lively evenings. It is small and crowded in high season, so it suits less well if you want space or quiet, unless you stay on a beach away from the village.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay on Koh Phi Phi?</h2>\n<p>All rooms are on Phi Phi Don. Choose between being in the middle of things and being a boat ride away.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What it is like</th><th>Getting there from the pier</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Tonsai village</td><td>The main village by the pier, with shops, restaurants and dive shops</td><td>On foot</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Loh Dalum</td><td>Across the isthmus from Tonsai; shallow water by day and the main nightlife beach after dark</td><td>A short walk</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long Beach</td><td>Wider and quieter, with clear water and views of Phi Phi Leh</td><td>About 20–30 min on foot, or a short longtail ride</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Laem Tong</td><td>A beach in the north, away from the village</td><td>About 30–40 min by longtail</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Loh Bagao</td><td>A sheltered bay on the north-east side</td><td>About 20–30 min by longtail</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>If you stay near Loh Dalum, expect noise from the bars late into the night.</p>\n<h2>How do you get to Koh Phi Phi?</h2>\n<p>There is no airport, so everyone arrives by boat at Tonsai Pier. The usual route from Bangkok is a flight to Phuket or Krabi, then a ferry.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From Phuket:</strong> ferries leave Rassada Pier, which is about 40–55 minutes from Phuket airport. The fastest boats take about an hour and slower ferries about two hours. Sailings run from about 9am to 5pm.</li>\n<li><strong>From Krabi:</strong> ferries leave Klong Jilad Pier, about 30 minutes from Krabi airport, and take about one to two hours. Most sailings leave between 9am and mid-afternoon.</li>\n<li><strong>From Ao Nang and Railay:</strong> boats run from Nopparat Thara pier near Ao Nang and from Railay East.</li>\n<li><strong>From Koh Lanta:</strong> speedboats run year-round. Slower ferries run only from November to April.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Book a day ahead in December and January, when ferries fill up.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Koh Phi Phi?</h2>\n<p>Phi Phi Don has no cars, so most places in the village are within walking distance. Longtail boats work as taxis to the outer beaches, costing roughly 100–400 baht depending on the distance.</p>\n<h2>What are the best things to do on Koh Phi Phi?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Walk to the viewpoint.</strong> The climb starts behind the village and takes about 25 minutes at a steady pace, with steep stairs. It looks over Tonsai and Loh Dalum bays. A small entry fee is charged near the top. Go early in the morning or near sunset, and bring water.</li>\n<li><strong>Visit Phi Phi Leh by boat.</strong> Longtail and speedboat trips go to Maya Bay and Pileh Lagoon, where swimming and snorkelling are allowed. Book on the island so you can check the weather and crowds first.</li>\n<li><strong>Snorkel at Bamboo Island.</strong> North of the main islands, with white sand and clear, shallow water that suits beginners.</li>\n<li><strong>See Monkey Beach.</strong> A small cove on Phi Phi Don, reached by kayak or longtail. The macaques can be aggressive if fed or approached, so keep your visit short and your bags closed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Early boats tend to be quieter, with calmer water and better visibility.</p>\n<h2>What are the rules at Maya Bay?</h2>\n<p>Maya Bay, on Phi Phi Leh, was closed from 2018 to 2022 to let the reef recover. It reopened with strict rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No swimming.</strong> You may wade up to your knees at the shoreline. The bay is home to blacktip reef sharks.</li>\n<li><strong>No boats in the bay.</strong> Boats moor at a pontoon in Loh Samah Bay, at the back of the island, and you walk a short boardwalk to the beach.</li>\n<li><strong>Limited time.</strong> The beach is open from about 7am to 6pm, numbers are capped at up to 375 people an hour, and groups usually get about an hour.</li>\n<li><strong>Annual closure.</strong> Maya Bay closes every year from 1 August to 30 September, along with Loh Samah and Loh Ko. In 2026 it reopened on 1 October.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Go early, between about 7am and 9am, before most day-trip boats from Phuket arrive.</p>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Koh Phi Phi?</h2>\n<p>The best months are November to April, with the busiest period from December to February. Book rooms two to three months ahead for that peak. The rainy season runs from May to October, with the most rain around September. It brings swell, rougher crossings and fewer boats, and ferries may be cancelled or delayed when the sea is rough, especially from June to September. Build a spare day into your plans.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Carry cash.</strong> You pay a 20 baht cleaning fee in cash at Tonsai Pier on arrival, and many small businesses do not take cards. ATMs in Tonsai charge foreign cards about 250–350 baht.</li>\n<li><strong>Budget for the park fee.</strong> Trips to Phi Phi Leh, Maya Bay and Bamboo Island are in Hat Noppharat Thara–Mu Ko Phi Phi National Park. Foreign adults pay 400 baht and children 200 baht. Check whether your tour includes it.</li>\n<li><strong>Use reef-safe sunscreen.</strong> Sunscreens containing oxybenzone, octinoxate, 4-MBC or butylparaben are banned in Thai national parks, with fines of up to 100,000 baht.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> if you want sleep as well as a night out, stay on Long Beach or further north and take a longtail into the village for the evening.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Ferry times and fares are approximate and change with the season and sea conditions; check timetables before you travel. Maya Bay rules, closures and national park fees are set by the park and can change.', 'sources': [('Koh Phi Phi travel guide (Direct Ferries)', 'https://www.directferries.com/blog/koh-phi-phi-travel-guide'), ('Koh Phi Phi guide (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/guides/koh-phi-phi-guide'), ('Maya Bay reopens on 1 October 2026 (Tisland Travel)', 'https://www.tisland.travel/en/blog/maya-bay-otkryvaetsya-1-oktyabrya-2026'), ('Thailand monsoon season: when it rains and where to go (Wego)', 'https://blog.wego.com/thailand-monsoon-season/'), ('Koh Phi Phi to Phuket ferry (Direct Ferries)', 'https://www.directferries.com/koh_phi_phi_phuket_ferry.htm'), ('Phuket to Phi Phi ferry transfer (Trazy)', 'https://www.trazy.com/experience/detail/phuket-phi-phi-islands-ferry-transfer-jet-cruiser')], 'photo': 33053823},
    {'slug': 'krabi-railay', 'name': 'Krabi & Railay', 'coast': 'andaman', 'region': 'Krabi province', 'tagline': 'Limestone cliffs, climbing and island hopping', 'summary': "Krabi's coast is mainland, but Railay can only be reached by boat. Stay in Ao Nang for choice and island trips, or Railay and Tonsai for cliffs and climbing.", 'best_months': 'Nov–Apr', 'getting_there': 'Fly to Krabi, then a 10–15 min longtail', 'best_for': ['Climbing', 'Island hopping', 'Viewpoints'], 'short': 'Krabi & Railay Travel Guide', 'title': 'Krabi & Railay Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Krabi and Railay travel guide: Ao Nang, Railay, Tonsai and Klong Muang compared, flights and longtail boats, island-hopping trips, park fees and when to go.', 'category': 'Islands · Andaman coast', 'teaser': 'Ao Nang, Railay, Tonsai and Klong Muang, boats and island trips.', 'image': 'isl-krabi-railay', 'image_alt': 'Palm trees and longtail boats on a beach below limestone cliffs in Krabi', 'stats': [('~90 min', 'Flight from Bangkok to Krabi'), ('10–15 min', 'Longtail from Ao Nang to Railay West'), ('700+', 'Bolted climbing routes at Railay and Tonsai'), ('May–Oct', 'Rainy season, when longtails may be delayed')], 'body': '\n<p>Krabi is not an island. It is a stretch of mainland coast in Krabi province, known for limestone cliffs rising out of the sea and dozens of islands just offshore. Railay, the best-known part, is a peninsula cut off by cliffs, with no roads or cars, so you arrive by longtail boat. Ao Nang, the main beach town, has the most rooms and boat trips. The area suits climbers, island hoppers and anyone who wants scenery more than nightlife. It suits less well if you want a good swimming beach on your doorstep in Ao Nang, where most swimming is done after a boat ride.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay in Krabi and Railay?</h2>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What it is like</th><th>Best for</th><th>From Krabi airport</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Ao Nang</td><td>The main resort town, with the most rooms and restaurants; boats leave from the beach</td><td>First visits, island trips</td><td>35–45 min by road</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nopparat Thara</td><td>The long beach past Ao Nang\'s headland, with soft sand, trees and fewer bars</td><td>Families, calmer evenings</td><td>Just beyond Ao Nang</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Railay</td><td>A car-free peninsula of cliffs and four beaches; rooms sell out first in high season</td><td>Scenery, climbing, couples</td><td>To Ao Nang, then a 10–15 min longtail</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tonsai</td><td>The climbers\' bay next to Railay; basic rooms on generator power, no ATM</td><td>Climbing, low budgets</td><td>Longtail from Ao Nang or Nopparat Thara</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Klong Muang</td><td>A quiet line of four- and five-star resorts facing the islands, with no strip</td><td>Families, resort stays</td><td>About 45 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Krabi Town</td><td>A river town with markets and cheap food; no beach</td><td>Budget, first or last night</td><td>About 12 km</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2>What are Railay\'s beaches like?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Railay West:</strong> the main swimming beach, facing the sunset. Longtails from Ao Nang land here, so swim at either end, away from the boats.</li>\n<li><strong>Railay East:</strong> mangrove and mudflat, not a swimming beach. It has cheaper rooms, bars, climbing schools and a floating pier for boats from Krabi Town and Ao Nam Mao. It is about five minutes\' walk from Railay West.</li>\n<li><strong>Phra Nang:</strong> at the southern tip, with clear water and the Princess Cave shrine. Tour boats crowd it from about 11am to 3pm, so go before 10am or late in the afternoon.</li>\n<li><strong>Tonsai:</strong> the next bay north, a 15–20 minute jungle walk from Railay West, or along the rocks at low tide.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How do you get to Krabi and Railay?</h2>\n<p>Flights from Bangkok\'s Suvarnabhumi and Don Mueang airports take about 90 minutes. Check which Bangkok airport your ticket uses, as they are far apart. Overnight buses from Bangkok take about 15 and a half hours. The cheapest route is a train to Surat Thani and a minivan on to Krabi, which takes 12 hours or more.</p>\n<p>From Krabi airport, shared vans to Ao Nang cost about 150–200 baht a seat and take 35–45 minutes. A taxi costs about 700–1,000 baht. Minivans link Krabi and Ao Nang with Phuket in about two to three and a half hours.</p>\n<p>To reach Railay:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From Ao Nang:</strong> shared longtails to Railay West cost 100 baht one way, take 10–15 minutes and leave when about eight passengers have gathered, roughly 8am to 6pm. Expect a knee-deep wade at both ends. In rough seas or at low tide, boats may leave from Nopparat Thara instead.</li>\n<li><strong>From Ao Nam Mao:</strong> speedboats run every 30 minutes from 8am to 6pm to Railay East\'s floating pier, for 150 baht. This is the steadiest crossing in rough weather and runs year-round.</li>\n<li><strong>From Krabi Town:</strong> shared longtails from Chao Fah pier take about 45 minutes and have no fixed timetable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ferries to Koh Phi Phi leave from Klong Jilad Pier near Krabi Town, Nopparat Thara and Railay East.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around?</h2>\n<p>Songthaews run between Ao Nang and Krabi Town, taking 25–30 minutes and costing 60 baht by day and 80 baht after 6pm. Klong Muang has no reliable public transport, so use resort shuttles, Grab or taxis. Railay and Tonsai are on foot only.</p>\n<h2>What are the best things to do?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Climb.</strong> Railay and Tonsai have more than 700 bolted routes, from beginner slabs to hard overhangs. Half-day beginner courses cost about 1,500 baht with gear, and no experience is needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Hike to the viewpoint.</strong> The trail starts on the path between Railay East and Phra Nang and climbs a red-mud slope with fixed ropes for 10–20 minutes. Beyond it, a harder route drops to the Sa Phra Nang lagoon. Only try it in dry weather, in shoes with good grip.</li>\n<li><strong>Take the Four Islands trip.</strong> Tours from Ao Nang usually visit Phra Nang Cave beach, Koh Tub and Koh Mor (joined by a sandbank), Koh Poda and Chicken Island.</li>\n<li><strong>Visit the Hong Islands.</strong> About 30–40 minutes from Ao Nang by longtail, with a lagoon inside a ring of cliffs. Go early, before about 9.30am, to beat the crowds.</li>\n<li><strong>Go to Koh Phi Phi.</strong> Speedboats from Ao Nang take under an hour.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit?</h2>\n<p>The dry season runs from November to April. January and February are the driest months. The rainy season runs from May to October, with the most rain around September and October. Swell on the Ao Nang side can delay or cancel longtails then, so the Ao Nam Mao speedboat is the safer way into Railay. Island tours still run in the rainy season.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bring cash to Railay.</strong> There are only a few ATMs on the peninsula and none at Tonsai. Thai ATMs charge foreign cards about 250–350 baht a withdrawal.</li>\n<li><strong>Budget for park fees.</strong> The Four Islands trip carries a national park fee of 200 baht for adults and 100 baht for children, paid in cash. The Hong Islands fee is 300 baht for adults and 150 baht for children. Tours often do not include them.</li>\n<li><strong>Expect basic power at Tonsai.</strong> Generators mean daytime power cuts, cold water and mostly no air conditioning.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> stay in Ao Nang for the widest choice and easiest island trips, then spend a night or two in Railay to see the cliffs after the day-trippers leave.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Boat times and fares are approximate and change with the tide, weather and season; check before you travel. National park fees are set by the park and can change.', 'sources': [('Railay Beach guide (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/guides/railay-beach-guide'), ('Where to stay in Krabi (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/guides/where-to-stay-in-krabi'), ('Ao Nang to Railay Beach (Backpackers Wanderlust)', 'https://www.backpackerswanderlust.com/railay-beach-to-ao-nang-thailand/'), ('Bangkok to Krabi (A Social Nomad)', 'https://asocialnomad.com/thailand/bangkok-to-krabi/'), ('Krabi 4 Islands tour by speedboat (Trazy)', 'https://www.trazy.com/experience/detail/krabi-4-islands-speedboat-tour'), ('Mu Koh Hong, Krabi (Pickyourtrail)', 'https://pickyourtrail.com/attractions/krabi/mu-koh-hong-krabi')], 'photo': 14738148},
    {'slug': 'phuket', 'name': 'Phuket', 'coast': 'andaman', 'region': 'Phuket province', 'tagline': "Thailand's largest island, beaches for every taste", 'summary': "Thailand's largest island has a beach for most styles of trip, from busy Patong to quiet national park sand. It is the easiest Andaman island to reach and a base for boat trips.", 'best_months': 'Nov–Apr', 'getting_there': 'Flight from Bangkok, about 1 h 20 min', 'best_for': ['Beaches', 'Day trips', 'Nightlife'], 'short': 'Phuket Travel Guide', 'title': 'Phuket Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Phuket travel guide: Patong, Kata, Karon, Kamala, Bang Tao and Nai Harn compared, flights and airport transfers, getting around, day trips and when to go.', 'category': 'Islands · Andaman coast', 'teaser': 'Which Phuket beach to pick, how to get around and when to go.', 'image': 'isl-phuket', 'image_alt': 'A small beach cove surrounded by green hills in Phuket', 'stats': [('~1 h 20 min', 'Flight from Bangkok to Phuket'), ('~1 h', 'Taxi from the airport to Patong, Kata or Karon'), ('100 baht', 'Smart Bus flat fare from the airport to Rawai'), ('May–Oct', 'Rainy season, with rough seas and red flag days')], 'body': '\n<p>Phuket is Thailand\'s largest island and the easiest place on the Andaman coast to reach. It is big enough to feel like several places at once: a party strip at Patong, family beaches further south, quiet resort bays in the north and an old town with no beach at all. It suits first-time visitors, families and anyone who wants plenty of choice and easy boat trips. It suits less well if you want somewhere small and quiet, unless you choose your beach carefully.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Which beach should you stay on in Phuket?</h2>\n<p>Most visitors stay on the west coast, where the main beaches are. Pick your beach first, then your room, because moving between them takes time.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What it is like</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Patong</td><td>About 3 km of sand and the island\'s busiest hub, with Bangla Road\'s nightlife. Quieter at the north end</td><td>Nightlife, convenience</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Karon</td><td>About 3.5 km long and wide, so it rarely feels full</td><td>Couples, long walks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kata</td><td>About 1.5 km, relaxed, calm in high season and good for surfing in low season</td><td>Families, first visits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kata Noi</td><td>A small, quiet bay of about 700–800 m</td><td>A quiet stay</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nai Harn</td><td>A short beach backed by green hills, with a less commercial feel</td><td>Couples, nature</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kamala</td><td>About 2 km, calm and mostly family-friendly</td><td>Families, calm stays</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Surin</td><td>About 1 km of soft sand; upscale with few budget options</td><td>Couples, higher budgets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bang Tao</td><td>About 8 km: beach clubs in the south, resorts in the middle, a quiet north end</td><td>Long stays</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mai Khao &amp; Nai Yang</td><td>Undeveloped national park beaches near the airport; Mai Khao is the longest on the island</td><td>Solitude, early flights</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phuket Old Town</td><td>Sino-Portuguese shophouses, cafés and markets; no beach</td><td>Food, culture, budget</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2>How do you get to Phuket?</h2>\n<p>Most people fly. Flights from Bangkok\'s Suvarnabhumi and Don Mueang airports take about 1 hour 20 minutes. Buses run from Bangkok\'s Southern Bus Terminal, usually in the evening, and take 12 to 14 hours.</p>\n<p>Phuket is also linked by road to Krabi, with minivans taking about two to three and a half hours from Krabi Town, Ao Nang or Krabi Airport. Ferries and speedboats run between Rassada Pier and Koh Phi Phi.</p>\n<p>The airport is in the north of the island, about 40 km from Patong. A taxi takes about an hour to Patong, Kata, Karon or Chalong, and costs roughly 700–900 baht to Patong including the 100 baht airport fee. The Smart Bus is cheaper, at a flat 100 baht, but takes about 90 minutes to Patong.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Phuket?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Smart Bus:</strong> runs from the airport down the west coast to Rawai, with more than 25 stops and a flat 100 baht fare. A separate route links Phuket Bus Terminal 1 with Patong for 50 baht.</li>\n<li><strong>Ride-hailing apps:</strong> Grab is the most reliable and the only app with official pickup points at the airport. Bolt is usually cheaper but waits can be longer away from the main beaches.</li>\n<li><strong>Tuk-tuks:</strong> not metered, so agree the fare before you get in. Apps are almost always cheaper for the same trip.</li>\n<li><strong>Blue songthaews:</strong> run from the Phuket Town market out to individual beaches, about every 30 minutes from 6am to 6pm, for 30–50 baht. They do not run between beaches.</li>\n<li><strong>Scooters:</strong> you need a motorcycle category on your licence and International Driving Permit. Helmets are compulsory, with a 2,000 baht fine. Inexperienced riders are better off renting a car.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What are the best things to do in Phuket?</h2>\n<p>The beaches come first. For snorkelling from the shore, try the small cove of Ya Nui or Freedom Beach, reached only by boat or a steep trail. Mai Khao, inside a national park, is a sea turtle nesting site and the place to go for space.</p>\n<p>On land, spend two or three hours in Phuket Old Town, around Thalang Road and Soi Romanee. A walking street market runs on Thalang Road on Sunday evenings. The Big Buddha, a 45-metre white marble statue on Nakkerd Hill, is open from 9am to 6pm, but check access before you go, as it changed after a fatal landslide in 2024. Cover shoulders and knees. Promthep Cape, at the southern tip, is free and busy at sunset, so arrive about 30 minutes early.</p>\n<p>Boat trips are the other main draw:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phang Nga Bay:</strong> limestone islands, sea caves and mangroves, often with a stop at James Bond Island and Koh Panyee.</li>\n<li><strong>Koh Phi Phi:</strong> day tours usually include Maya Bay and Pileh Lagoon. Maya Bay normally closes from 1 August to 30 September.</li>\n<li><strong>Racha Islands and Shark Point:</strong> popular for diving.</li>\n<li><strong>Similan Islands:</strong> normally open from 15 October to 15 May. Most trips leave from piers near Khao Lak, north of Phuket.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Phuket?</h2>\n<p>The dry season runs roughly from November to April, and December is close to ideal. The southwest monsoon brings rain from May to October, with regular afternoon storms from June to August and the most rain around September. Mornings are often clear even in the wet months, so plan boat trips early in the day.</p>\n<p>The low season also changes the sea. Kata gets good surf, but Karon, Kata, Surin, Bang Tao and Mai Khao can have strong waves and rip currents. Ferries to Phi Phi and other islands may be cancelled or delayed in rough weather, especially from June to September.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Respect the red flags.</strong> West coast beaches can have rip currents in the rainy season, so follow the flag warnings.</li>\n<li><strong>Check park closures before booking tours.</strong> Maya Bay and the Similan Islands both close for part of the year.</li>\n<li><strong>Get insurance that covers riding.</strong> Thai insurers do not cover damage to rental bikes.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> if you want nightlife, stay in Patong and take day trips elsewhere. If you want quiet, stay in Kata, Kamala, Nai Harn or the north and visit Patong for an evening.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Travel times and fares are approximate and depend on traffic, sea conditions and the service; check flight, bus and boat times before you travel. Park opening dates and access to attractions can change.', 'sources': [('How to get to Phuket (Aussie Divers Phuket)', 'https://www.aussiediversphuket.com/how-to-get-to-phuket/'), ('The best beaches in Phuket (Phuket 101)', 'https://www.phuket101.net/zh-CN/phuket-best-beaches/'), ('Getting around Phuket (Phuket 101)', 'https://www.phuket101.net/getting-around-phuket/'), ('Travelling from Phuket Airport to Patong (Inside Our Suitcase)', 'https://insideoursuitcase.com/phuket-airport-to-patong/'), ('Things to do in Phuket (Thai Holiday Guide)', 'https://www.thaiholidayguide.com/things-to-do-in-phuket/'), ('Thailand monsoon season: when it rains and where to go (Wego)', 'https://blog.wego.com/thailand-monsoon-season/')], 'photo': 16141092},
    {'slug': 'koh-lanta', 'name': 'Koh Lanta', 'coast': 'andaman', 'region': 'Krabi province', 'tagline': 'Long beaches and a slower pace', 'summary': 'A long, narrow island south of Krabi with a string of west-coast beaches. Calmer than Phuket or Phi Phi, it suits families, couples and divers.', 'best_months': 'Nov–Apr', 'getting_there': 'Van and car ferry from Krabi airport, about 2–2.5 h', 'best_for': ['Families', 'Diving', 'Quiet beaches'], 'short': 'Koh Lanta Travel Guide', 'title': 'Koh Lanta Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Koh Lanta guide: its beaches compared, vans and boats from Krabi, Phi Phi and Ao Nang, getting around, diving and day trips, and the best months to visit.', 'category': 'Islands · Andaman coast', 'teaser': 'Long sandy beaches, easy road access and a calmer pace south of Krabi.', 'image': 'isl-koh-lanta', 'image_alt': 'Aerial view of a beach and green hills on Koh Lanta', 'stats': [('~30 km', 'Length of Koh Lanta, from Saladan in the north to the national park'), ('2–2.5 h', 'Van from Krabi airport to Koh Lanta, plus any car-ferry queue'), ('Nov–Apr', 'Main season, with dry weather and calm seas'), ('16 May–14 Oct', 'Usual closure of the Koh Rok visitor area')], 'body': '\n<p>Koh Lanta is a long, narrow island south of Krabi, about 30 km from top to bottom. A single road runs down the west coast past a chain of sandy beaches, from busy Khlong Dao in the north to quiet bays near the national park in the south. It is calmer than Phuket or Koh Phi Phi, and it can be reached by road and car ferry, so it suits families, couples and divers who want space more than nightlife.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay on Koh Lanta?</h2>\n<p>Pick a beach by how much you want to drive. The north has the most shops and transport. The south is quieter and more expensive, but you will need a scooter or taxis to get anywhere.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Best for</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Khlong Dao</td><td>Families, first visits</td><td>Long, flat beach near Saladan; shops, pharmacies, ATMs and the most tuk-tuks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Long Beach (Phra Ae)</td><td>Young travellers, remote workers</td><td>About 3 km of sand, beach bars and cafés; the island\'s liveliest stretch</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Khlong Khong</td><td>Budget stays, parties</td><td>Cheapest area; rocks show at low tide; a scooter helps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Khlong Nin</td><td>Couples</td><td>Quieter, with bars on the sand and better swimming</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kantiang Bay and the south</td><td>Quiet, higher-end stays</td><td>Sheltered bay, fewer hotels; hilly road with long unlit stretches</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Old Town</td><td>An afternoon or dinner</td><td>Stilted wooden houses on the east coast; no beach</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Saladan, at the northern tip, is where boats dock and where most practical errands are done. Without a scooter, Khlong Dao and the north end of Long Beach are the easiest bases.</p>\n<h2>How do you get to Koh Lanta?</h2>\n<p>There is no airport on the island. From Bangkok, fly to Krabi, which takes about 1 hour 20 minutes, then continue by road. Krabi airport is about 77 km from Saladan.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Van from Krabi airport:</strong> about 2 to 2.5 hours and roughly ฿400–540, with departures from about 08:00 to 20:30. Vans drop at your hotel.</li>\n<li><strong>Van from Krabi Town:</strong> about 2.5 to 3 hours, roughly hourly through the day.</li>\n<li><strong>Private car:</strong> about ฿2,100–3,300 and around 2 hours door to door. Worth it if you land after about 20:00.</li>\n<li><strong>Boat from Ao Nang or Railay:</strong> speedboats run all year, taking about 1 hour 10 minutes to 1 hour 50 minutes. More sailings are added from November to April.</li>\n<li><strong>Boat from Koh Phi Phi:</strong> about 30 to 45 minutes by speedboat or around an hour by ferry. Sailings are fewer from May to October.</li>\n<li><strong>Boat from Koh Lipe:</strong> about 3 hours by speedboat in season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The road route uses a short car ferry from Hua Hin pier on the mainland to Lanta Noi, then the Siri Lanta bridge to Lanta Yai, where the beaches are. In December and January the ferry queue can add an hour or more. A bridge to the mainland has been contracted, but the ferry is expected to run until around 2029.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Koh Lanta?</h2>\n<p>A scooter costs about ฿300 a day and makes the southern beaches easy to reach. Check your licence, helmet and insurance first, and take care on the unlit hills at night. Tuk-tuks run along the west-coast road, often priced per person and by distance, so agree the fare before you set off. Several trips in a day can cost more than a scooter.</p>\n<h2>What are the best beaches and things to do?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Beaches:</strong> Khlong Dao for gentle, shallow water; Long Beach for space to walk; Kantiang Bay for a sheltered cove. Check warning flags, as swimming conditions change with the waves.</li>\n<li><strong>Mu Ko Lanta National Park:</strong> at the southern tip, with a short trail to the lighthouse at Laem Tanod. It is a good place for sunset.</li>\n<li><strong>Lanta Old Town:</strong> on the east coast, with stilted wooden houses, Chinese shrines, a mosque and waterfront restaurants.</li>\n<li><strong>Koh Rok:</strong> the most popular snorkelling trip, with clear water and coral. Speedboat trips often add Koh Haa.</li>\n<li><strong>Four Islands tour:</strong> usually Koh Ngai, Koh Muk with its Emerald Cave, Koh Kradan and Koh Chueak.</li>\n<li><strong>Diving:</strong> Lanta is a base for Hin Daeng and Hin Muang, deep pinnacles with walls dropping to 60 m or more, where manta rays and whale sharks are sometimes seen. Koh Haa has caverns and coral gardens suited to beginners.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Koh Lanta?</h2>\n<p>November to April is the main season, with dry weather and calm seas. December and January are busiest, and rooms fill up around Christmas. The diving season matches it, running from November to April.</p>\n<p>May to October is the monsoon on the Andaman coast. The sea is rough, many bungalows close, and most boat trips stop. September and October are the wettest months. The Koh Rok visitor area normally closes from 16 May to 14 October. Vans run all year, but boats from Krabi Town only run from November to April.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bring a card, but expect fees.</strong> ATMs are easy to find in the north, but each withdrawal carries a fee of about ฿250, plus your own bank\'s charge.</li>\n<li><strong>Allow time for the ferry.</strong> In peak season, build the car-ferry queue into your plans for flights.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep boat plans flexible.</strong> Rough seas can cancel any boat on the worst days.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> if you will not rent a scooter, stay in Khlong Dao or at the north end of Long Beach and book boat trips with hotel pickup.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Van, ferry and boat times and fares are approximate and change with the season and sea conditions; check timetables before you travel. The car-ferry queue can add an hour or more in December and January.', 'sources': [('Krabi to Koh Lanta (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/guides/krabi-to-koh-lanta'), ('Things to do in Koh Lanta (Thai Holiday Guide)', 'https://www.thaiholidayguide.com/things-to-do-in-koh-lanta/'), ('Where to stay in Koh Lanta (Descubriendo Viajes)', 'https://descubriendoviajes.com/en/thailand/where-to-stay-in-koh-lanta/'), ('Ferry from Koh Phi Phi to Koh Lanta (Go-Ferry)', 'https://www.go-ferry.com/routes/thailand-ferry-routes/ferry-koh-phi-phi-tonsai-pier-koh-lanta-saladan-pier'), ('Diving Koh Lanta: Hin Daeng, Hin Muang and Koh Haa (Divernet)', 'https://divernet.com/world-dives/asia/diving-koh-lanta-thailand/'), ('Bangkok to Krabi transport guide (Expatolife)', 'https://www.expatolife.com/bangkok-to-krabi-thailand/')], 'photo': 1647064},
    {'slug': 'koh-yao-noi', 'name': 'Koh Yao Noi', 'coast': 'andaman', 'region': 'Phang Nga province', 'tagline': 'Village life in Phang Nga Bay', 'summary': 'A quiet, mostly Muslim island between Phuket and Krabi, with karst views across Phang Nga Bay. It suits slow travel, climbing and boat trips more than nightlife.', 'best_months': 'Nov–Apr', 'getting_there': "Speedboat from Phuket's Bang Rong pier, about 30–40 min", 'best_for': ['Slow travel', 'Rock climbing', 'Island hopping'], 'short': 'Koh Yao Noi Travel Guide', 'title': 'Koh Yao Noi Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Koh Yao Noi guide: its east-coast beaches compared, boats from Phuket and Krabi, getting around by scooter, Phang Nga Bay trips, local customs and when to go.', 'category': 'Islands · Andaman coast', 'teaser': 'A quiet Muslim island in Phang Nga Bay, a short boat ride from Phuket.', 'image': 'isl-koh-yao-noi', 'image_alt': 'Limestone islands across Phang Nga Bay, where Koh Yao Noi lies', 'stats': [('~12 km', 'Length of Koh Yao Noi, about 5 km wide'), ('30–40 min', 'Speedboat from Bang Rong pier in Phuket to Manoh pier'), ('~20 min', 'Speedboat from Ao Thalane in Krabi to Tha Khao pier'), ('฿300', 'National park fee for adults at Koh Hong')], 'body': '\n<p>Koh Yao Noi sits in Phang Nga Bay, between Phuket and Krabi, facing a skyline of limestone islands. It is about 12 km long and home to around 5,000 people, mostly Muslim fishing and farming families. There are rubber plantations, rice fields and a handful of small resorts, but few bars and no big town. It suits travellers who want a slow, rural island with easy boat trips, not a party or a long walk on white sand.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay on Koh Yao Noi?</h2>\n<p>Most places to stay are on the east coast, which looks across the bay to Krabi\'s karsts. The beaches are tidal: at low tide the sea can pull back a long way, leaving sand and stones, so a room with a pool is worth having.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Best for</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Pasai (southeast)</td><td>First visits</td><td>About 2 km, the longest beach; bungalows and cafés nearby; very shallow at low tide</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Klong Jark (east)</td><td>A quieter stay</td><td>About 1.5 km, restaurants at the south end; swim at high tide</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tha Khao (northeast)</td><td>Yoga, Krabi boats</td><td>About 1 km beside the village and pier; a sandbank links it to Koh Nui at low tide</td></tr>\n<tr><td>North tip</td><td>Seclusion</td><td>A few villa resorts; tracks are dirt and you will need transport</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Pasai and Klong Jark are about five minutes apart by scooter, and most of the island\'s restaurants are along this stretch.</p>\n<h2>How do you get to Koh Yao Noi?</h2>\n<p>There is no airport on the island. From Bangkok, fly to Phuket or to Krabi, which is about 1 hour 20 minutes, and take a boat from there.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From Phuket:</strong> Bang Rong pier, on Phuket\'s northeast coast, is about 25 to 40 minutes by car from Phuket airport. Speedboats reach Manoh pier on Koh Yao Noi in 30 to 40 minutes, for about ฿300–400. Slower boats take about an hour. The last public boats leave around 17:00.</li>\n<li><strong>From Krabi:</strong> speedboats run from Ao Thalane pier to Tha Khao pier in about 20 minutes, for about ฿300, roughly from 08:00 to 17:45. A longtail leaves at 13:00 for about ฿200. Ao Thalane is under an hour by road from Krabi airport.</li>\n<li><strong>From Ao Nang:</strong> a speedboat from Nopparat Thara pier costs about ฿650.</li>\n<li><strong>From Koh Yao Yai:</strong> longtails cross from Manoh pier in 10 to 15 minutes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If your flight lands late in the afternoon, you may miss the last public boat. Plan for a night on the mainland, or ask your hotel about a private transfer.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Koh Yao Noi?</h2>\n<p>A scooter is the usual choice, at about ฿250–350 a day. One main road circles the island, and a full loop without stops takes about an hour. Roads are dark after sunset, and some beach tracks are steep and rocky. There is no bus. Local taxis meet boats at Manoh pier, and a shared songthaew from the pier to a hotel costs about ฿100 per person.</p>\n<h2>What are the best things to do on Koh Yao Noi?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Island hopping in Phang Nga Bay:</strong> longtail trips go to Koh Hong, with its enclosed lagoon, and Koh Lao Lading. The Koh Hong lagoon can only be entered at high tide. The national park fee is ฿300 for adults and ฿150 for children, paid in cash. Go early, before the speedboats from Phuket arrive.</li>\n<li><strong>Private longtail:</strong> about ฿2,500 for a four-island trip, usually leaving around 09:00 and returning around 14:00.</li>\n<li><strong>James Bond Island:</strong> a full-day Phang Nga Bay trip also visits Koh Panyee and sea caves for kayaking. Expect crowds and a ฿300 park fee.</li>\n<li><strong>Rock climbing:</strong> there are more than 150 routes, including deep-water soloing. Guides and gear are available on the island, and the best months are November to March.</li>\n<li><strong>Kayaking:</strong> kayaks can be rented for short paddles to nearby islets. Return before low tide, or you will carry the kayak across a long stretch of sand.</li>\n<li><strong>Sunsets:</strong> the east coast faces the sunrise, so head to the west or the south, such as the fishing huts at Laem Sai pier, for sunset.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Koh Yao Noi?</h2>\n<p>The dry season runs from November to April, and December to March has the calmest seas. May and October are shoulder months. From June to September there are roughly half as many sailings, crossings can be rough and some boats are cancelled. At the Phuket weather station, about 13 km away, February averages about 24 mm of rain and September about 350 mm.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dress modestly in the villages.</strong> Keep swimwear for the beach and resorts, and cover shoulders and knees near mosques.</li>\n<li><strong>Alcohol is limited.</strong> It is sold in resorts and tourist restaurants, mostly around Pasai and Klong Jark, but not everywhere. Ask before bringing your own.</li>\n<li><strong>Carry cash.</strong> There are a few ATMs in the main village, but they can run out on busy weekends, and many small places take cash only.</li>\n<li><strong>Evenings are quiet.</strong> There are only a couple of beach bars, so dinner is the main event.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> check the tide table before you plan a swim or a trip to the Koh Hong lagoon. High tide makes both far easier.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Boat times and fares are approximate and change with the season and sea conditions; sailings are reduced from June to September. Check timetables and national park fees before you travel.', 'sources': [('Koh Yao Noi travel guide (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/guides/koh-yao-noi-guide'), ('Things to do in Ko Yao Noi (Voyage Tips)', 'https://www.voyagetips.com/en/koh-yao-noi/'), ('How to get to Koh Yao Noi (Islaguru)', 'https://islaguru.com/articles/how-to-get-to-koh-yao-noi'), ('How to get here (Koyao Island Resort)', 'https://koyao.com/how-to-get-here'), ('Bangkok to Krabi transport guide (Expatolife)', 'https://www.expatolife.com/bangkok-to-krabi-thailand/')], 'photo': 11967509},
    {'slug': 'koh-lipe', 'name': 'Koh Lipe', 'coast': 'andaman', 'region': 'Satun province', 'tagline': 'A car-free island near Malaysia', 'summary': 'A small, car-free island in Tarutao National Marine Park, close to the Malaysian border. It suits snorkellers and walkers, but takes time to reach and is best in the dry season.', 'best_months': 'Nov–Apr', 'getting_there': 'Speedboat from Pak Bara, about 1.5 h, or Langkawi', 'best_for': ['Snorkelling', 'Island hopping', 'Walking'], 'short': 'Koh Lipe Travel Guide', 'title': 'Koh Lipe Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Koh Lipe guide: its beaches compared, boats from Pak Bara, Langkawi and Lanta, the Tarutao park fee, snorkelling trips, seasonal ferries and when to go there.', 'category': 'Islands · Andaman coast', 'teaser': 'A small, car-free island in Tarutao National Park, near the Malaysian border.', 'image': 'isl-koh-lipe', 'image_alt': 'Clear turquoise water over rocks and sand on Koh Lipe', 'stats': [('~2 km', 'Length of Koh Lipe, with no cars'), ('~90 min', 'Speedboat from Pak Bara pier on the mainland'), ('฿200', 'Tarutao National Park fee for foreign adults'), ('May–Oct', 'Monsoon, when only the Pak Bara boats run')], 'body': '\n<p>Koh Lipe is a small island, about 2 km long, in the far south of Thailand\'s Andaman coast, close to the border with Malaysia. It lies within Tarutao National Marine Park and was first settled by the Urak Lawoi sea people. There are no cars, and you can walk between the main beaches. It suits snorkellers and people happy to spend their days on boats, but it takes most of a day to reach and many businesses close in the monsoon.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay on Koh Lipe?</h2>\n<p>The island is small enough that every area is within walking distance of the others. Walking Street, lined with shops, restaurants and guesthouses, runs for about 20 minutes between Pattaya Beach and Sunrise Beach.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Best for</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Pattaya Beach (south)</td><td>First visits, eating out</td><td>Sheltered bay where boats arrive; the most bars, restaurants and boat traffic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sunrise Beach (east)</td><td>Swimming</td><td>The longest beach, with views of Koh Adang and longtails to it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sunset Beach (west)</td><td>A quieter stay</td><td>Smaller, with a few bungalows and bars</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Walking Street</td><td>Budget stays</td><td>Good-value guesthouses set back from the beach</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2>How do you get to Koh Lipe?</h2>\n<p>There is no airport and no bridge. Most people go through Pak Bara pier on the mainland.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From Bangkok:</strong> fly to Hat Yai, about 1 hour 30 minutes, then take a van to Pak Bara and a speedboat to the island. In total, plan on about 6 to 9 hours. Some airlines sell a single ticket covering flight, van and boat.</li>\n<li><strong>From Hat Yai:</strong> combined van and speedboat tickets cost about ฿800–950 and take about 3 to 5.5 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>From Pak Bara:</strong> speedboats take about 90 minutes and cost about ฿650–700. Most stop at Koh Tarutao or Koh Bulon on the way. In high season several boats run each day, mostly in the late morning and early afternoon. In low season there is usually only one.</li>\n<li><strong>From Langkawi, Malaysia:</strong> ferries take about 90 minutes and run from about mid-October to May. Arrive at least an hour early for passport control. You collect your passport from the Thai immigration desk at Pattaya Beach.</li>\n<li><strong>From Koh Lanta, Phi Phi or Phuket:</strong> seasonal speedboats take about 3 hours from Lanta, about 4 hours from Phi Phi and about 5 hours from Phuket.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Large boats stop at a floating pier offshore. A longtail to the beach costs about ฿50–70 unless it is included in your ticket. Depending on the tide, you may have to step into shallow water, so pack sensibly.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Koh Lipe?</h2>\n<p>On foot. There are no cars and visitors cannot rent scooters. Motorbikes with sidecars carry people and luggage for about ฿50 per person, and longtails between beaches cost about the same.</p>\n<h2>What are the best beaches and things to do?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Snorkelling trips:</strong> shared longtail trips cost about ฿600–800 per person and last 4 to 8 hours. A private longtail costs about ฿1,800–2,800. The inner-islands trip usually covers Koh Hin Ngam, Koh Yang and Koh Adang, with lunch on Koh Rawi.</li>\n<li><strong>Koh Hin Ngam:</strong> known for its striped stones. Taking them is banned.</li>\n<li><strong>Chado Cliff on Koh Adang:</strong> a steep trail of about 1 km, with ropes in places, takes 45 minutes to an hour to climb. The longtail from Sunrise Beach costs about ฿100 each way.</li>\n<li><strong>Koh Kra:</strong> a small rocky islet a few hundred metres off Sunrise Beach, reachable by rented kayak.</li>\n<li><strong>Diving:</strong> two dives cost about ฿3,000, and dive shops on Walking Street run trips to sites such as Stonehenge.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Koh Lipe?</h2>\n<p>November to April is the main season. December and January are the busiest and most expensive months, so book ahead.</p>\n<p>From May to October the monsoon brings rough seas. Only the Pak Bara boats run, crossings are less frequent and depend on the weather, and at least half of the restaurants, bars and tour shops close. The park\'s listing says most attractions close from 1 June to 30 September, and access to Koh Adang outside high season varies by year.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pay the park fee.</strong> Foreign adults pay ฿200 and children ฿100, usually at the pier. Keep the ticket for other islands in the park. Divers pay an extra ฿200 for each day of diving.</li>\n<li><strong>Bring cash.</strong> There are ATMs on Walking Street, but many places want cash and cards often carry a surcharge.</li>\n<li><strong>Leave plastic at home.</strong> The park has banned plastic bags and foam boxes.</li>\n<li><strong>Take care on speedboats.</strong> The crossing can be bumpy. If you have back or neck problems, choose a larger, slower boat.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> boats from Pak Bara mostly leave before mid-afternoon, so book an early flight to Hat Yai or spend a night near the pier.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Boat routes, times and fares are approximate and change with the season; many services stop from May to October. National park fees and closure dates can change, so check before you travel.', 'sources': [('Koh Lipe travel guide (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/guides/koh-lipe-travel-guide'), ('Tarutao National Marine Park (Thai National Parks)', 'https://static.thainationalparks.com/tarutao-national-marine-park'), ('Getting to Tarutao National Marine Park (Thai National Parks)', 'https://thainationalparks.com/tarutao-national-marine-park/get-there'), ('How to get to Koh Lipe (Love and Road)', 'https://loveandroad.com/how-to-get-to-koh-lipe-thailand/'), ('Ko Lipe (Thaizer)', 'https://www.thaizer.com/ko-lipe/'), ('Pak Bara to Koh Lipe (12Go)', 'https://12Go.asia/en/travel/pak-bara/koh-lipe')], 'photo': 2617548},
    {'slug': 'koh-samui', 'name': 'Koh Samui', 'coast': 'gulf', 'region': 'Surat Thani province', 'tagline': 'Gulf beaches, resorts and an airport', 'summary': "Thailand's second-largest island, with its own airport and the widest choice of beaches in the Gulf. Busy Chaweng and Lamai sit alongside quieter north and west coasts.", 'best_months': 'Dec–Apr', 'getting_there': 'Flight from Bangkok, about 1 h 30 min', 'best_for': ['Beach resorts', 'Families', 'Island day trips'], 'short': 'Koh Samui Travel Guide', 'title': 'Koh Samui Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Koh Samui guide: Chaweng, Lamai, Bophut and Maenam compared, flights and ferries from Bangkok, Ang Thong Marine Park trips, and when the Gulf rains come.', 'category': 'Islands · Gulf coast', 'teaser': "The Gulf's largest island, with an airport, many beaches and Ang Thong trips.", 'image': 'isl-koh-samui', 'image_alt': 'Rocky coastline and turquoise sea on Koh Samui', 'stats': [('~1 h 30 min', 'Flight from Bangkok Suvarnabhumi to Koh Samui'), ('30–50 min', 'Boat from Koh Samui to Koh Phangan'), ('300 baht', 'Ang Thong Marine Park fee for foreign adults'), ('Nov', 'Wettest month on Koh Samui')], 'body': '\n<p>Koh Samui is Thailand\'s second-largest island and the easiest of the Gulf islands to reach, with its own airport and nearly 40 beaches. It has the most choice of rooms, from busy Chaweng to quiet resorts on the west coast. It suits first-time visitors, families and couples who want comfort and short transfers more than a remote feel. It is also a good base for day trips to Ang Thong Marine Park and Koh Phangan.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay on Koh Samui?</h2>\n<p>Most visitors stay on the east and north coasts, where the main beaches are. The west and south are quieter, with fewer places to eat out.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What it is like</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Chaweng</td><td>The liveliest area, with a 7 km beach, beach clubs, shops and nightlife</td><td>First-timers, solo travellers, nightlife</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lamai</td><td>About 11 km south of Chaweng; some nightlife, but more relaxed</td><td>Those who find Chaweng too busy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bophut (Fisherman\'s Village)</td><td>Old wooden shophouses and a walking street market on Friday nights</td><td>Families, couples</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Maenam</td><td>A quieter northern beach about 5 km long, with local food</td><td>Couples, families, lower budgets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Choeng Mon</td><td>A secluded north-east area with upmarket spa resorts</td><td>Couples who want quiet near Chaweng</td></tr>\n<tr><td>West and south coasts</td><td>Quiet beaches such as Lipa Noi and Taling Ngam, with sunsets and no nightlife</td><td>Rest, wellness stays</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Hotel prices are highest from about November to March and can rise sharply in peak weeks. Book early for Christmas and New Year.</p>\n<h2>How do you get to Koh Samui?</h2>\n<p>The quickest way is to fly. Bangkok Airways runs several flights a day from Suvarnabhumi, taking about an hour and a half. The airport is on the east coast, close to Chaweng and Bophut. International flights are limited, so most people connect in Bangkok.</p>\n<p>A cheaper route is to fly to Surat Thani on the mainland and take a bus and ferry, which takes about four hours in total. Ferries leave from Donsak Pier. The Raja and Seatran car ferries take about an hour and a half; the Lomprayah catamaran takes about 45 minutes.</p>\n<p>Overland, an overnight sleeper train from Krung Thep Aphiwat Central station reaches Surat Thani in nine to ten hours. With the ferry, the whole journey takes ten to 12 hours. Buses from Bangkok take a similar time.</p>\n<p>Koh Samui is also the hub for the other Gulf islands. Boats to Koh Phangan take 30 to 50 minutes, depending on the pier. Boats to Koh Tao take about one and a half to three hours.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Koh Samui?</h2>\n<p>A ring road of about 50 km circles the island. Shared red pickups called songthaews run between the main beaches during the day, with fares of about 30 to 80 baht. They become rare after 8pm and do not serve the airport.</p>\n<p>Taxis have no meters, so agree the fare first. Expect about 250 to 300 baht from the airport to Chaweng. Grab works in Chaweng, Bophut, Lamai and Maenam, and is often cheaper, but is less reliable on the west coast. Scooters rent from about 150 to 200 baht a day. Helmets are required by law and police checkpoints are common.</p>\n<h2>What are the best things to do on Koh Samui?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ang Thong Marine Park:</strong> a group of 42 islands about 30 km from Samui. Day trips are the main reason many people visit. See below.</li>\n<li><strong>Big Buddha:</strong> a large golden statue on the small island of Koh Fan, linked to the north-east coast by road.</li>\n<li><strong>Wat Plai Laem:</strong> a temple near Bangrak known for its 18-armed statue of Guan Yin.</li>\n<li><strong>Hin Ta and Hin Yai:</strong> rock formations near Lamai, often visited with the Lamai viewpoints.</li>\n<li><strong>Fisherman\'s Village:</strong> the Friday night walking street in Bophut, with food and stalls.</li>\n<li><strong>Koh Phangan:</strong> 30 to 50 minutes away by boat, close enough for a day trip to its quieter beaches.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Is a day trip to Ang Thong Marine Park worth it?</h2>\n<p>Yes, if the weather is good. Mu Ko Ang Thong National Park covers 102 square kilometres of sea and limestone islands. Visits are only possible with licensed tour operators. Speedboats take about 45 to 60 minutes each way from Samui. Larger, slower boats also run.</p>\n<p>A typical tour includes snorkelling, kayaking and two short climbs. The trail to the Pha Jun Jaras viewpoint on Koh Wua Talap is about 500 metres uphill, steep, with ropes in places, and takes 25 to 30 minutes. On Koh Mae Ko, a stairway leads to a viewpoint over Thale Nai, a saltwater lake enclosed by cliffs.</p>\n<p>The park fee is 300 baht for foreign adults and 150 baht for children aged three to 14. Some tours do not include it, so carry cash. Wear proper shoes for the climbs.</p>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Koh Samui?</h2>\n<p>Samui\'s weather follows the Gulf pattern, not the Andaman one. December to February is dry, at about 24 to 30°C. March to May is hotter, with the most sunshine and the clearest sea. June to September brings frequent showers and occasional downpours.</p>\n<p>October and November are the wettest months, with November the peak. Seas can be rough and boat trips may be cancelled. Ang Thong Marine Park usually closes from 1 November to 15 December, though the dates depend on the weather. If you are travelling then, check before booking a tour.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plan around the rains.</strong> From October to December, keep a spare day in case boats do not sail.</li>\n<li><strong>Carry cash for the park.</strong> The Ang Thong fee is paid in cash and is often not in the tour price.</li>\n<li><strong>Agree taxi fares first.</strong> There are no meters, and fares from the west coast to Chaweng can reach 600 to 800 baht.</li>\n<li><strong>Ride with care.</strong> Wear a helmet on a scooter, and check your travel insurance covers it.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> choose your beach by how you plan to spend the evenings. Stay in Chaweng or Lamai for nightlife, Bophut or Maenam for a calmer base, and the west coast for quiet.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Flight and ferry times are approximate and depend on the operator, pier and sea conditions. Ang Thong Marine Park closure dates are set each year by the Department of National Parks and can change with the weather. Taxi, songthaew and scooter prices vary.', 'sources': [('How to get to Koh Samui, Koh Phangan and Koh Tao from Bangkok (Bangkok Airways)', 'https://blog.bangkokair.com/en/how-to-go-koh-samui-phangan-tao-from-bangkok/'), ('The best season to visit Koh Samui (Bangkok Airways)', 'https://blog.bangkokair.com/?p=12723'), ('Mu Ko Ang Thong National Park (Thai National Parks)', 'https://www.thainationalparks.com/mu-ko-ang-thong-national-park'), ('Ang Thong Marine Park speedboat tour from Koh Samui (Click2GoThailand)', 'https://www.click2gothailand.com/tours/00004'), ('Where to stay in Koh Samui: best areas (Global Gallivanting)', 'https://www.global-gallivanting.com/where-to-stay-in-koh-samui/'), ('Cheapest way to get around Koh Samui (Inside Samui)', 'https://www.insidesamui.com/getting-here/cheapest-way-to-get-around-koh-samui/')], 'photo': 5567082},
    {'slug': 'koh-phangan', 'name': 'Koh Phangan', 'coast': 'gulf', 'region': 'Surat Thani province', 'tagline': 'Full moon parties, yoga and quiet bays', 'summary': 'Known for the Full Moon Party at Haad Rin, but most of the island is calm. The west coast suits yoga and sunsets; the north and east have quiet bays.', 'best_months': 'Dec–Apr', 'getting_there': 'Boat from Koh Samui, about 30–50 min', 'best_for': ['Parties', 'Yoga', 'Quiet beaches'], 'short': 'Koh Phangan Travel Guide', 'title': 'Koh Phangan Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Koh Phangan guide: Haad Rin and the Full Moon Party, the quieter west and north coasts, boats from Samui and Surat Thani, 2027 party dates and the rainy season.', 'category': 'Islands · Gulf coast', 'teaser': 'Full moon parties in the south, yoga in the west, quiet bays elsewhere.', 'image': 'isl-koh-phangan', 'image_alt': 'A beach between green hills on Koh Phangan', 'stats': [('30–50 min', 'Boat from Koh Samui to Koh Phangan'), ('12–13 h', 'Train and ferry from Bangkok'), ('200 baht', 'Full Moon Party entry, paid in cash on the night'), ('627 m', "Khao Ra, the island's highest peak")], 'body': '\n<p>Koh Phangan sits between Koh Samui and Koh Tao in the Gulf of Thailand. It is best known for the Full Moon Party at Haad Rin, on the south-east tip. The rest of the island is much calmer, with yoga schools in the west, local villages in the north and secluded bays in the east. It suits partygoers and long-stay visitors, but also couples and families who stay away from Haad Rin.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay on Koh Phangan?</h2>\n<p>Roads are steep and some beaches are reached only by boat, so choose your area carefully.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What it is like</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Haad Rin</td><td>The party hub, with hostels and bars; calm outside full moon week</td><td>Partygoers, backpackers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thong Sala</td><td>The main town and ferry pier, with banks, shops and a night market</td><td>A central base, short stays</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Srithanu and the west coast</td><td>A string of beaches including Zen, Haad Yao and Salad, with yoga studios and sunsets</td><td>Yoga, longer stays, couples</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chaloklum (north)</td><td>A fishing village with few tourists, dive shops and mostly larger houses to rent</td><td>Groups, long stays</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bottle Beach (north-east)</td><td>An isolated bay reached only by boat, with few places to stay</td><td>Quiet and nature</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thong Nai Pan (north-east)</td><td>Two bays about 500 m apart; Noi is upmarket, Yai more casual</td><td>Couples, families</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>If you want the Full Moon Party without staying in Haad Rin, Ban Tai and Thong Sala have normal prices and shared pickups on party nights. Rooms in Haad Rin book out weeks ahead, and minimum stays of three to five nights are common.</p>\n<h2>How do you get to Koh Phangan?</h2>\n<p>The quickest route from Bangkok is to fly to Koh Samui, about an hour and a half, then take a boat. Crossings from Samui take 30 to 50 minutes, depending on the pier. Boats leave from Bangrak, near Chaweng, and from Pralarn. Most arrive at Thong Sala, but the Haad Rin Queen runs from Bangrak straight to Haad Rin.</p>\n<p>A cheaper route is to fly or take the train to Surat Thani and catch a ferry from Donsak Pier. The car ferries take about two and a half hours and the Lomprayah catamaran about an hour and a half. By overnight train and ferry, the whole trip from Bangkok takes 12 to 13 hours; by bus and ferry, 11 to 12 hours.</p>\n<p>From Koh Tao, boats to Thong Sala take about one to one and three-quarter hours. Book a few days ahead around full moon dates, Christmas and New Year, when boats fill up.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Koh Phangan?</h2>\n<p>Shared pickups called songthaews wait at Thong Sala pier and in Haad Rin. The busiest route links the two.</p>\n<p>Scooters rent for about 150 to 300 baht a day. The roads are steep and bumpy, so only ride if you are experienced. Wear a helmet and proper shoes, and check that your licence and insurance cover you. Longtail boats run from Haad Rin, Thong Sala and most beaches, and are the only way to reach Bottle Beach, Haad Yuan and Haad Thian.</p>\n<h2>What is the Full Moon Party like?</h2>\n<p>The party takes over Haad Rin Nok, also called Sunrise Beach, once a month. Around a dozen bars each run their own sound system. The beach fills from about 10pm and the party peaks between 1am and 3am. Entry is 200 baht, paid in cash at the gate. Online "tickets" are usually transfer or hostel packages, not beach entry.</p>\n<p>The party is held on the full moon, but moves by a day or more when that falls on a Buddhist holiday or another national event. The Haad Rin organisers announce the dates. The provisional dates for 2027 are:</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Date</th><th>Month</th><th>Date</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>January</td><td>Fri 22</td><td>July</td><td>Tue 20</td></tr>\n<tr><td>February</td><td>Mon 22</td><td>August</td><td>Tue 17</td></tr>\n<tr><td>March</td><td>Mon 22</td><td>September</td><td>Wed 15</td></tr>\n<tr><td>April</td><td>Wed 21</td><td>October</td><td>Sat 16</td></tr>\n<tr><td>May</td><td>Fri 21</td><td>November</td><td>Sat 13</td></tr>\n<tr><td>June</td><td>Sat 19</td><td>December</td><td>Mon 13</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A New Year countdown party is also held on 31 December. Some sources give 21 February and 20 May instead, so reconfirm about a month before you travel. Ticketed Halfmoon and jungle parties run near Ban Tai at other points in the month.</p>\n<h2>What else is there to do on Koh Phangan?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Snorkelling at Koh Ma:</strong> a small island off Mae Haad in the north-west, joined by a sandbar you can walk at low tide, with coral and fish. Bring your own mask.</li>\n<li><strong>Diving:</strong> dive schools in Thong Sala, Haad Yao, Chaloklum and Haad Rin run trips to Sail Rock, a site for more experienced divers.</li>\n<li><strong>Waterfalls:</strong> Than Sadet, inside a national park, and Phaeng, a two-tier fall. Flow varies with the season; September to February is usually best.</li>\n<li><strong>Viewpoints:</strong> Khao Ra is the highest peak at 627 m, reached by a long trail. West-coast bars are popular for sunset.</li>\n<li><strong>Yoga:</strong> most schools are in Srithanu, with daily classes and teacher training.</li>\n<li><strong>Ang Thong Marine Park:</strong> speedboat day trips also leave from Phangan, with snorkelling, kayaking and a viewpoint climb.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Koh Phangan?</h2>\n<p>Koh Phangan has the same weather as its Gulf neighbours. December to February is dry and warm. March to May is hotter, with calm seas and the clearest water. June to September brings frequent showers and occasional downpours.</p>\n<p>October and November are the wettest months, peaking in November. Seas can be rough and boats may be cancelled. Ang Thong Marine Park usually closes from 1 November to 15 December.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wear shoes at the party.</strong> Broken glass in the sand is the most common injury.</li>\n<li><strong>Watch your drink.</strong> Buy your own bucket and see it poured. Avoid swimming after dark.</li>\n<li><strong>Drug laws are strict.</strong> Plain-clothes police attend the party.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not ride after drinking.</strong> Take a songthaew or taxi back instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Emergency numbers:</strong> Tourist Police 1155; Koh Phangan Hospital +66 77 377 034.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> if you want one big night, stay on the west coast or in Thong Sala and travel to Haad Rin for the party. You get the quiet side of the island for the rest of the week.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Full Moon Party dates for 2027 are provisional and are set by the Haad Rin organisers; sources differ for February and May, so reconfirm before booking. Ferry times are approximate and depend on the operator, pier and sea conditions. National park closure dates can change with the weather.', 'sources': [('Full Moon Party, Koh Phangan (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/guides/full-moon-party-koh-phangan'), ('Full Moon Party schedule (THAIest)', 'https://thaiest.com/blog/full-moon-party-schedule'), ('Thailand Full Moon Party (Direct Ferries)', 'https://www.directferries.com/thailand/things-to-do/itineraries/full-moon-party'), ('How to get to Koh Samui, Koh Phangan and Koh Tao from Bangkok (Bangkok Airways)', 'https://blog.bangkokair.com/en/how-to-go-koh-samui-phangan-tao-from-bangkok/'), ('Where to stay on Koh Phangan (Time Travel Turtle)', 'https://www.timetravelturtle.com/thailand/where-to-stay-on-koh-phangan/'), ('Koh Phangan things to do (THAIest)', 'https://thaiest.com/blog/koh-phangan-things-to-do')], 'photo': 32107700},
    {'slug': 'koh-tao', 'name': 'Koh Tao', 'coast': 'gulf', 'region': 'Surat Thani province', 'tagline': 'Dive courses, granite bays and Nang Yuan', 'summary': 'A small, hilly Gulf island built around scuba diving, with shallow reefs, quiet southern bays and a busy beach strip at Sairee. Many people come for a three-day course and stay longer.', 'best_months': 'Feb–Sep', 'getting_there': 'Ferry from Samui, Phangan or Chumphon, about 1.5–2 h', 'best_for': ['Diving courses', 'Snorkelling', 'Viewpoints'], 'short': 'Koh Tao Travel Guide', 'title': 'Koh Tao Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Koh Tao travel guide: where to stay from Sairee to Chalok, ferries from Samui, Phangan and Chumphon, dive courses, Koh Nang Yuan and the best months to visit.', 'category': 'Islands · Gulf coast', 'teaser': 'Learn to dive, snorkel off Nang Yuan and pick the right bay.', 'image': 'isl-koh-tao', 'image_alt': 'Looking out from the viewpoint over Koh Nang Yuan at sunset, off Koh Tao', 'stats': [('~2 h', 'Catamaran from Koh Samui to Koh Tao'), ('~1 h 30 min', 'Fast ferry from Chumphon to Koh Tao'), ('฿11,000–12,500', 'Typical Open Water dive course in 2026'), ('Nov', 'Usual peak of the monsoon on Koh Tao')], 'body': '\n<p>Koh Tao is the smallest of the three main Gulf islands, north of Koh Samui and Koh Phangan. It is known above all for scuba diving. Dive schools line the west coast, courses are good value, and calm, shallow reefs make it an easy place to learn. Away from Sairee Beach the island is hilly and quiet, with small bays at the end of steep roads. It suits divers, snorkellers and anyone wanting a slower island than Samui, but not those after long, wide beaches.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay on Koh Tao?</h2>\n<p>Most people stay on the west coast, between Sairee and Mae Haad. The south and east have quieter bays but are harder to reach.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Best for</th><th>What it is like</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Sairee Beach</td><td>First visits, nightlife, divers</td><td>The longest beach, with bars, restaurants and rooms for most budgets</td><td>About a 15-minute walk from the pier</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mae Haad</td><td>Divers, no scooter</td><td>The village around the main pier, with dive shops and cheaper rooms</td><td>Quiet in the evening; the easiest place to find transport</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chalok Baan Kao</td><td>A calmer base near town</td><td>A bay of about 500 metres on the south coast, with low-key bars</td><td>Under ten minutes from Mae Haad by road</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Southern and eastern bays (Tanote, Shark Bay, Aow Leuk)</td><td>Snorkelling, couples</td><td>Small coves, mostly at the bottom of steep hills</td><td>Prices tend to be higher; going in and out is tiring</td></tr>\n<tr><td>North coast</td><td>Seclusion</td><td>Hillside bungalows on a rocky, less developed coast</td><td>All within about 5 km of the pier, but by steep roads</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2>How do you get to Koh Tao?</h2>\n<p>Koh Tao has no airport, so every route ends with a boat. Most ferries arrive at Mae Haad on the west coast.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From Koh Samui:</strong> the Lomprayah catamaran takes about two hours and the Lomlahkkhirin speedboat about an hour and a half, both from about 700 baht. Slower Songserm ferries take up to three hours or more. Bangkok Airways flies Bangkok to Samui in about an hour and a half.</li>\n<li><strong>From Koh Phangan:</strong> boats from Thong Sala take about one to one and three-quarter hours, from about 450 to 600 baht.</li>\n<li><strong>From Chumphon:</strong> Lomprayah and Boonsiri run fast ferries twice a day, for about 750 baht one way. The crossing takes about an hour and a half. Buses from Bangkok to Chumphon take about six to eight hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Train and ferry from Bangkok:</strong> the overnight sleeper to Surat Thani takes nine to ten hours from Krung Thep Aphiwat Central. A catamaran from Donsak pier then takes about three and a quarter hours. Allow 13 to 14 hours in all.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Book ahead around holidays and Koh Phangan\'s full moon parties.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Koh Tao?</h2>\n<p>There is no bus service, and no Grab or Bolt. Taxis are pickup trucks with bench seats. There is no meter, so agree the price first. Short hops around Sairee and Mae Haad cost about 50 to 100 baht per person by day, and more at night. Longtail boats run to bays that are hard to reach by road.</p>\n<p>Scooters cost about 200 to 250 baht a day, often with a cash deposit of 3,000 to 5,000 baht. Think carefully before you hire one. The roads are steep, with blind bends and loose sand, and they get slippery in the rain. Do not learn to ride here. Walking works well between Sairee and Mae Haad, which takes about 20 to 30 minutes along the coast.</p>\n<h2>Is Koh Tao good for learning to dive?</h2>\n<p>Yes. Dive schools are everywhere and the reefs are calm and shallow. An Open Water course takes three to four days and costs about 11,000 to 12,500 baht in 2026. Some schools add a free dorm bed while you train. Certified divers pay about 800 to 1,200 baht per fun dive. Deeper sites such as Chumphon Pinnacle, Southwest Pinnacle and Sail Rock cost more than standard sites.</p>\n<p>The island\'s shape helps. One side is usually sheltered, so training and reef dives can carry on through most of the year. Whale sharks are most often seen from February to May.</p>\n<h2>What are the best things to do on Koh Tao?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Koh Nang Yuan:</strong> small islands joined by a sandbar, about 500 metres off the north-west coast. Longtail boats from Sairee take about 15 minutes. Climb the short, steep path to the viewpoint, then snorkel the shallow reef beside the sandbar.</li>\n<li><strong>Snorkelling bays:</strong> Tanote Bay on the east coast is known for snorkelling. Shared half-day longtail snorkel trips cost about 800 to 1,200 baht per person.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On Koh Nang Yuan, plastic bottles, cans, fins and water shoes are not allowed, to protect the reef. Staff check bags on arrival. Day visitors pay an entry fee in cash, reported at 250 baht for adults and 100 baht for children, and must leave by late afternoon.</p>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Koh Tao?</h2>\n<p>Koh Tao follows the Gulf pattern, not the Andaman one. The monsoon usually arrives in late October or November and can last until around Christmas, sometimes into January. November is generally the worst month. Strong winds can make crossings rough and cloud the water.</p>\n<p>February to May is the hot, dry season, with water up to about 30°C and very good visibility. February to September is the best period for diving, with visibility often over 20 metres. July and August are busy but usually calm. September and October are quieter and still often good.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bring cash.</strong> Taxis, longtail boats and the Nang Yuan fee are paid in cash.</li>\n<li><strong>Save water.</strong> Fresh water is scarce in the hot, dry months.</li>\n<li><strong>Allow a spare day.</strong> Monsoon crossings can be rough, so avoid tight flight connections.</li>\n<li><strong>Check your insurance.</strong> Make sure it covers diving, and riding a scooter if you plan to.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> if you plan to dive, choose the school before the room. Many schools offer a discount on rooms nearby, and staying close saves early-morning trips across the hills.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Ferry times and fares, dive prices and the Koh Nang Yuan fee change often; check with operators before you travel. Weather varies from year to year.', 'sources': [('How to get to Koh Samui, Koh Phangan and Koh Tao from Bangkok (Bangkok Airways)', 'https://blog.bangkokair.com/en/how-to-go-koh-samui-phangan-tao-from-bangkok/'), ('Where to stay on Koh Tao (Time Travel Turtle)', 'https://www.timetravelturtle.com/thailand/where-to-stay-on-koh-tao/'), ('How to get around Koh Tao (Lorenzigo)', 'https://lorenzigo.com/how-to-get-around-koh-tao/'), ('Budget guide 2026: how much is diving on Koh Tao? (Big Blue Diving)', 'https://www.bigbluediving.com/blog/budget-guide-2026-how-much-is-diving-on-koh-tao/'), ('Koh Tao weather and climate (Black Turtle Dive)', 'https://www.blackturtledive.com/koh-tao/weather/'), ('How to visit Koh Nang Yuan island viewpoint (The World Travel Guy)', 'https://theworldtravelguy.com/koh-nang-yuan-island-viewpoint-koh-tao-thailand/')], 'photo': 3417733},
    {'slug': 'koh-samet', 'name': 'Koh Samet', 'coast': 'east', 'region': 'Rayong province', 'tagline': 'White sand within easy reach of Bangkok', 'summary': 'The nearest beach island to Bangkok, reached by bus and a short boat ride from Ban Phe. Small, sandy and part of a national park, it is busy at weekends and quiet midweek.', 'best_months': 'Nov–Apr', 'getting_there': 'Bus to Ban Phe, then ferry, about 4–5 h', 'best_for': ['Short breaks', 'Beaches', 'Sunsets'], 'short': 'Koh Samet Travel Guide', 'title': 'Koh Samet Travel Guide: Where to Stay, Getting There, Beaches and When to Go (2027)', 'description': 'Koh Samet travel guide: where to stay from Sai Kaew to Ao Prao, buses and boats from Bangkok, the national park fee, island day trips and the best time to go.', 'category': 'Islands · Eastern Gulf', 'teaser': 'The closest beach island to Bangkok, best visited on a weekday.', 'image': 'isl-koh-samet', 'image_alt': 'A beach and rocky shore on the Rayong coast, where Koh Samet lies', 'stats': [('~3–4 h', "Bus from Bangkok's Ekkamai terminal to Ban Phe pier"), ('~40 min', 'Wooden ferry from Ban Phe to Na Dan pier'), ('฿200', 'National park fee for foreign adults'), ('~7 km', 'Length of the island from end to end')], 'body': '\n<p>Koh Samet is the closest beach island to Bangkok, and the easiest to visit without flying. It is small, about 7 km from end to end, and lies off the coast of Rayong province. The whole island is part of Khao Laem Ya–Mu Ko Samet National Park, so every visitor pays a park fee. Beaches of fine white sand run down the east coast, with quieter coves further south. It suits a short break from the capital, couples and families. Thai weekend crowds are part of the picture, so it is calmer from Monday to Thursday.</p>\n<!--TOC-->\n<h2>Where should you stay on Koh Samet?</h2>\n<p>Most rooms are on the east coast, between the pier and Ao Wong Duean. The further south you go, the quieter and less developed it becomes.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Beach</th><th>Best for</th><th>What it is like</th><th>Good to know</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Hat Sai Kaew</td><td>Convenience, nightlife</td><td>The main beach, about 1 km of white sand with shallow water</td><td>Closest to Na Dan pier; busiest at weekends; has ATMs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ao Hin Khok &amp; Ao Phai</td><td>Budget travellers, evenings out</td><td>Smaller bays just south of Sai Kaew, calm by day</td><td>Fire shows on the beach at night</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ao Wong Duean</td><td>Families, couples</td><td>A long, curved bay with calm water</td><td>Restaurants and shops, but quieter than Sai Kaew</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ao Prao</td><td>Quiet stays, sunsets</td><td>The main west-coast beach, with a few upmarket resorts</td><td>Few independent places to eat; the island\'s best sunsets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ao Wai &amp; Ao Kiu</td><td>Seclusion</td><td>Small southern coves with little development</td><td>Limited facilities; further from the pier</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rooms are mostly cheaper and more varied around Sai Kaew and Ao Phai. Expect higher prices at weekends and on Thai public holidays.</p>\n<h2>How do you get to Koh Samet from Bangkok?</h2>\n<p>The trip has two parts: road to Ban Phe pier on the mainland, then a boat to Na Dan pier at the north of the island. Allow four to five hours door to door.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bus:</strong> Cherdchai buses leave Bangkok\'s Eastern Bus Terminal at Ekkamai and take about three to four hours to Ban Phe. The fare is about 190 baht. Minivans also run, for about 200 to 300 baht.</li>\n<li><strong>Bus and boat from the airport:</strong> a combined service leaves Suvarnabhumi and Khao San Road in the morning and reaches Na Dan in about five and a half hours, for 750 baht.</li>\n<li><strong>Private car:</strong> about two and a quarter hours to Ban Phe in light traffic. From Pattaya, Ban Phe is about 81 km away, a little over an hour by car.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>From Ban Phe, the wooden ferry takes about 40 minutes and runs roughly hourly through the day, for 50 to 70 baht one way. A catamaran takes about ten minutes for 150 baht. Shared speedboats take about 15 minutes and can drop you at some beaches, for 200 to 500 baht depending on where you go. Boats stop in the late afternoon, so aim to reach Ban Phe by early afternoon.</p>\n<h2>How do you get around Koh Samet?</h2>\n<p>Green songthaews, shared pickup trucks, wait at Na Dan pier and run to the beaches. Shared rides to Sai Kaew and Ao Phai cost about 20 to 60 baht. Hiring one to a southern beach costs about 200 to 600 baht. The east-coast beaches are joined by a coastal path, so you can walk between them. Scooters cost about 300 baht a day, but some inland roads are partly dirt and speed bumps are hard to see at night.</p>\n<h2>What are the best things to do on Koh Samet?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Walk the east coast:</strong> follow the path south from Sai Kaew through Ao Hin Khok and Ao Phai, with small coves and headlands along the way.</li>\n<li><strong>Sunset at Ao Prao:</strong> cross to the west coast in the late afternoon. There is also a hilltop viewpoint that looks over both sunrise and sunset.</li>\n<li><strong>Snorkelling:</strong> the rocks around Ao Prao are the main snorkelling spot. Ao Wai is shallow and quieter.</li>\n<li><strong>Island-hopping by boat:</strong> day trips of about six hours visit nearby islands such as Koh Kudi and Koh Thalu, with snorkelling stops. Tours are cancelled in bad weather.</li>\n<li><strong>Short forest walks:</strong> informal trails link Ao Wai, Ao Thian and Ao Pakarang in the south.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When is the best time to visit Koh Samet?</h2>\n<p>November to April is the driest period, with calm seas and the most sunshine. November to February is the high season. March and April are hot and good for swimming. May to October is the rainy season. Seas can be rough at times, but Koh Samet gets less rain than the Andaman islands, so it can be visited all year. Jellyfish sometimes appear in the rainy months.</p>\n<p>The timing within the week matters as much as the month. The island fills up on weekends and Thai long holidays, and is noticeably quieter and cheaper from Monday to Thursday.</p>\n<h2>What should you know before you go?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Park fee:</strong> foreign adults pay 200 baht and children 100 baht, in cash, at the checkpoint after landing. Thai nationals pay 40 baht. Keep the ticket, as it is sometimes checked again.</li>\n<li><strong>Bring cash:</strong> you need it for the park fee and boats. Sai Kaew has ATMs, but the southern beaches have few services.</li>\n<li><strong>Book weekends early:</strong> crowds peak on weekends and Thai public holidays.</li>\n<li><strong>Watch the last boat:</strong> ferries stop in the late afternoon, and timings change, so check before you set out.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> go between Monday and Thursday if you can. The island is quieter, rooms are cheaper and the beaches near the pier are less crowded.</p></div>\n', 'checked_note': 'Facts checked October 2026. Bus times, boat fares and the national park fee can change; check with operators and at the pier before you travel. Weather varies from year to year.', 'sources': [('Bangkok to Koh Samet: practical guide (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/guides/bangkok-to-koh-samet'), ('Koh Samet destination guide (Visit Thailand Today)', 'https://www.visitthailandtoday.com/destinations/koh-samet'), ('Ultimate Koh Samet travel guide (Thom Travels)', 'https://thomtravels.mypixieset.com/blog/ultimate-koh-samet-travel-guide/'), ('Koh Samet (Thailand Magazine)', 'https://thailandmagazine.com/koh-samet/'), ('Khao Laem Ya national park (Travelfish)', 'https://www.travelfish.org/sight_profile/thailand/eastern_thailand/rayong/ban_phe/3662'), ('Ko Samet 5 or 6 islands snorkeling tour (Happy to Visit)', 'https://happytovisit.com/ko-samet-5-or-6-islands-snorkeling-tour-with-lunch-option-2/')], 'photo': 14160137},
]
